  "preamble": "Replacement of the postalveolar affricate [tʃ] by the glottal fricative [h] removes all oral articulation, keeping only voiceless frication. Several Tupi-Guarani languages of Brazil changed Proto-Tupi-Guarani *t͡ʃ to h, as in *t͡ʃɨ 'mother' > Guajajára ihy and *jat͡ʃɨ 'moon' > Urubú-Kaapor jahy, while Old Tupi kept a sibilant (sy, îasy). Cases that pass through a fricative stage (tʃ > ʃ > h, as in Terena) or start from a different affricate (*ts > h) belong in those cells instead.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Debuccalization, Deaffrication",
